WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.266
Here's the whole path in one picture.

2
00:00:02.266 --> 00:00:06.863
A branch client's query goes from the
SD-WAN edge to an agency resolver,

3
00:00:06.863 --> 00:00:11.380
then through the DDI policy plane,
then to each cloud's private zones.

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.472
Internet-bound lookups leave
through CISA's Protective DNS.

5
00:00:15.472 --> 00:00:19.053
Five decisions carry almost
all the operational risk.

6
00:00:19.053 --> 00:00:21.822
The edge points at a real agency resolver.

7
00:00:21.822 --> 00:00:24.447
That resolver is always the first hop.

8
00:00:24.447 --> 00:00:27.724
The policy plane holds
every cloud forwarding rule.

9
00:00:27.724 --> 00:00:33.277
AD DNS is inventoried and rebuilt
before any domain controller goes.

10
00:00:33.277 --> 00:00:36.223
And revocation endpoints
resolve from everywhere.

11
00:00:36.223 --> 00:00:39.871
What makes these dangerous is
that each one fails silently.

12
00:00:39.871 --> 00:00:44.519
The query still resolves, just outside
policy or to the wrong address.

13
00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:47.684
So each decision comes with
a check, run against the live

14
00:00:47.684 --> 00:00:51.834
configuration rather than the design
document, before the next change.

15
00:00:51.834 --> 00:00:54.050
Which brings us to who owns all this.
